\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Argentina seeks blns in aid to survive crisis. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Gilbert Le Gras and Anna Willard \par \uc2 1,112 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2002\par 01:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES/WASHINGTON, Feb 13 (Reuters) - On its second day of talks with multilateral lenders in Washington, Argentina on Wednesday tentatively estimated it needs $22 billion to $23 billion in aid to get back on its feet, but the IMF said it was "premature" to talk numbers. \par \par \uc2  "Normally these crises require about 12 percent of economic output, which in our case is 22 to 23 billion dollars, and in some cases they have required more," said Argentine Deputy Economy Minister Jorge Todesca in Buenos Aires. "That doesn't mean we're negotiating these numbers with the agencies," he told a local radio station. \par \par \uc2  But while Todesca said that no "specific number" was yet on the table in negotiations with the International Monetary Fund that he predicted could drag on for four to six weeks, his tentative estimate jibed with those of other Argentine officials. \par \par \uc2  In Washington, Economy Minister Jorge Remes Lenicov said an IMF mission may travel to Argentina as soon as next week to formally start negotiations on a new economic program. \par \par \uc2  "We have a good image of having met people who want to help Argentina and we are prepared to continue making all the changes needed to revert this crisis," Remes said, adding the government still needed to clarify its position on tax reform, bank sector restructuring and revenue sharing with provinces. \par \par \uc2  IMF Managing Director Horst Koehler said both sides agreed to the need for a comprehensive plan for sustainable growth. \par \par \uc2  "This was a good start to a new relationship, and there was broad agreement on the analysis of the economic and social situation," Koehler said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  The Argentine government on Wednesday heeded IMF and U.S. Treasury warnings against bankruptcy law reforms passed by Congress last month and vetoed a controversial clause that would have forced banks to write down loans that were not renegotiated within 90 days. \par \par \uc2  It also decreed a 20 percent tax on oil exports. \par \par \uc2  News of the cordial talks combined with the successful flotation of the peso earlier this week provided Argentines a breather from relentlessly news of recession. \par \par \uc2  Since December when deadly riots forced out two presidents in succession, shaking one of Latin America's firmest democracies to its core, Argentina has defaulted on part of its $141 billion public debt and devalued its peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after being fixed at par to the dollar for over a decade. \par \par \uc2  Officials say President Eduardo Duhalde is leery of sending a "shopping list" of funding needs, especially since his country swallowed up billions of dollars in assistance last year and still could not avert the massive debt default in December. \par \par \uc2  Argentina also overshot its IMF-agreed public sector deficit goal last year of 6.5 billion pesos by 2.587 billion pesos, the Economy Ministry said late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Argentines' savings are still restricted by limits on cash withdrawals, imposed to stop a record run on banks last year. Since the public sector and local businesses have no access to credit, many shops and factories have been forced to close, driving up unemployment. \par \par \uc2  LIFE ON HOLD \par \par \uc2  While the peso finds its feet and President Duhalde's government tries to patch things up with wary multilateral lenders, everyday life is on hold for millions of people. \par \par \uc2  With unemployment now estimated at 22 percent conversation here centers on jobs lost and dollar savings forcibly converted to pesos and locked up in the banks. For many, personal plans like weddings and birthdays have been canceled or scaled down. \par \par \uc2  "I had saved up $7,000 to move out on my husband and get a divorce," laments Rosa, an unhappy Buenos Aires hairdresser. \par \par \uc2  As he bought dollars at a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  house, Antonio, a 75-year-old Argentine pensioner who lives in Spain, said that he had only come to see his daughter for Christmas, but his cash was stuck in the bank and he could not buy a ticket home. \par \par \uc2  "I was supposed to have left a 1-1/2 months ago," he said. \par \par \uc2  Others scramble to get around the economic shackles. \par \par \uc2  Speculators, who locals call "arbolitos," or little trees, stand on street corners offering black-market dollars to those who cannot face the long lines at exchange bureaus. \par \par \uc2  But the lack of cash to buy dollars meant the feared dive in the peso's value with Monday's full flotation did not materialize. The country had a dual system for just over a month with a fixed peso for exports and a floating peso for the public. \par \par \uc2  Confounding forecasts, the peso has strengthened from its lows right after devaluation to close on Wednesday at 1.95 to the dollar on the retail market and 1.98 for large-scale deals. Some had predicted it would fall as low as 5 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But traders agree the relative stability is due to the cash crunch rather than any newfound faith in the peso from people who have always stashed away their savings in U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  HYPERINFLATION FEARS \par \par \uc2  While prices on imported goods rise, fueling fears of a return to the hyperinflation of the 1980s, barter markets offering goods and services from food to plumbing have spread. \par \par \uc2  Bankers say Argentina has a month to secure foreign aid before it falls into the grip of hyperinflation. \par \par \uc2  January's 2.3 percent rise in consumer prices in a country accustomed to zero inflation for years and mindful of the daily and even hourly price rises seen back in the 1980s, showed how panic-inducing a real bout of hyperinflation would be. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the economy is at a standstill. The run on the banks until cash limits were imposed in early December emptied the system of a quarter of its deposits. \par \par \uc2  The problems have also spilled over into neighboring Uruguay, where knock-on liquidity woes at Banco Galicia Uruguay, a bank owned by \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's largest private-sector bank, prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Ratings agency Fitch reported that losses to the Argentine banking system in 2001 may exceed 18 percent of gross domestic product, or 50 billion pesos. \par \par \uc2  In a television interview late Tuesday, Duhalde put a brave face on the daily protests against the cash crunch and growing unemployment, saying: "It's ridiculous to believe a president will step down because of protests. I'm even less likely to step down if the crisis gets worse." \par \par \uc2  Attributing his determination to his Basque roots, the stocky former vice president from the Peronist Party promised that within "two months these things that bother people like roadblocks will disappear and things will calm down." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020214dy2e000h6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
