\par\par \b \uc2  Argentina says needs over $22 bln to survive crisis. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Stephen Brown \par \uc2 877 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2002\par 18:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, Argentina, Feb 13 (Reuters) - On the second day of talks in Washington, Argentina on Wednesday tentatively estimated its needs at $22 billion to $23 billion, saying fresh aid could put its crippled economy and banking system back on their feet. \par \par \uc2  Argentine Deputy Economy Minister Jorge Todesca said in Buenos Aires that no "specific number" was on the table yet in negotiations with the International Monetary Fund that he predicted would drag on for four to six weeks. \par \par \uc2  Officials have said President Eduardo Duhalde is wary of sending Washington a "shopping list" of funding needs, after his country swallowed up billions of dollars of mutilateral assistance last year without averting a damaging debt default in December. \par \par \uc2  But Todesca's estimate number jived with private estimates by officials. "Normally these crises require about 12 percent of economic output, which in our case is 22 to 23 billion dollars, and in some cases they have required more. That doesn't mean we're negotiating these numbers with the agencies," he told a local radio station. \par \par \uc2  Argentine and U.S. officials said the talks with the IMF, U.S. Treasury, World Bank and InterAmerican Development Bank had gotten off to a good start, combining with the successful flotation of the peso ARSB=) this week, giving Argentina a breather from relentlessly bleak news of recession and misery. \par \par \uc2  Since deadly riots forced out two presidents in succession in December, shaking one of Latin America's firmest democracies to its core, Argentina has defaulted on part of its $141 billion in public debt and devalued its peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after being fixed at par to the dollar for over a decade. \par \par \uc2  Access to Argentines' savings is still blocked by strict limits on cash withdrawals, imposed to stop a record run on banks last year. Since the public sector and local businesses have no access to credit, shops and factories have been forced to close, driving up unemployment. \par \par \uc2  LIFE ON HOLD \par \par \uc2  While the peso finds its feet and President Duhalde's government tries to patch things up with wary multilateral lenders, everyday life is on hold for millions of people. \par \par \uc2  Conversation here centers on jobs lost - with unemployment now estimated at 22 percent - and dollar savings forcibly converted to pesos and locked up in the banks, meaning personal plans like weddings and birthdays are canceled or scaled down. \par \par \uc2  "I had saved up $7,000 to move out on my husband and get a divorce," laments Rosa, an unhappy Buenos Aires hairdresser. \par \par \uc2  Others scramble to get around the economic shackles. \par \par \uc2  Speculators, who locals call "arbolitos," or little trees, stand on street corners offering black-market dollars to those who cannot face waiting in line for hours at \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  houses. \par \par \uc2  But the locals don't have enough cash in pesos to snap up the dollars available and produce the dive in peso value that had been feared with Monday's full flotation. The country had a dual system for just over a month with a fixed peso for exports and a floating peso for the public. \par \par \uc2  Confounding forecasts, the peso has strengthened from its lows immediately after devaluation to sell on Wednesday at 2.0 to the dollar on the retail market and 1.95 for large-scale deals. Some had predicted it would fall as low as 5 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But traders agree the relative stability is due to the cash crunch rather than any newfound faith in the peso from a population which has always stashed away its savings in U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  While prices on imported goods rise, fueling fears of a return to the hyperinflation of the 1980s, barter markets offering goods and services from food to plumbing have spread like wildfire. \par \par \uc2  Bankers say Argentina has a month to secure foreign aid before it falls into the grip of hyperinflation. \par \par \uc2  January's 2.3 percent rise in consumer prices in a country accustomed to zero inflation for many years and mindful of the daily and even hourly price rises seen back in the 1980s, showed how panic-inducing a real bout of hyperinflation would be. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the orthodox economy is at a standstill. The run on the banks until cash limits were imposed in early December emptied the system of a quarter of deposits. \par \par \uc2  That concern has spilled over into neighboring Uruguay, a regional tax haven where knock-on liquidity woes at Banco Galicia Uruguay, a bank owned by the same shareholders as \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's largest private-sector bank, prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Ratings agency Fitch reported on Wednesday that losses to the Argentine banking system in 2001 may exceed 18 percent of gross domestic product, or 50 billion pesos. \par \par \uc2  In a television interview late Tuesday, Duhalde put a brave face on the daily protests against the cash crunch and growing unemployment, saying: "It's ridiculous to believe a president will step down because of protests. I'm even less likely to step down if the crisis gets worse." \par \par \uc2  Attributing his determination to his Basque roots, the stocky former vice president from the Peronist Party promised that within "two months these things that bother people like roadblocks will disappear and things will calm down." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020213dy2d00xho\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
