\par\par \b \uc2  Argentine cenbank sells dollars to support peso. \b0\par\par\uc2 192 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2002\par 14:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Feb 1 (Reuters) - The Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the 14th straight session on Friday, selling U.S. dollars at 1.75 pesos each to prop up the peso, the bank said. \par \par \uc2  The bank did not say how many dollars it sold. \par \par \uc2  The peso traded at an average 1.93/1.95 per dollar for large-scale transactions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday. The 1.95 peso sell rate equals a 49 percent fall from the pre-devaluation one-to-one peso-to-dollar rate maintained for a decade. \par \par \uc2  Argentina has an official fixed exchange rate of 1.40 pesos per dollar for foreign trade and priority transactions as designated by the government, and an unofficial floating peso for cash deals. The government plans to ditch the dual exchange rate and fully float the peso in the coming months. \par \par \uc2  The floating-rate peso has weakened as companies and people have sought the safe haven of dollars due to fears that the peso will depreciate even further amid a deep recession now in its fourth year. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020201dy2100pxz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
