\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2 's Peso Ends At ARS1.95/Dlr; Ctrl Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 358 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2002\par 21:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso lost ground against the dollar Thursday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offered greenbacks at their highest rate since the peso began free trading Jan. 11. \par \par \uc2  Commercial banks quoted the peso closing at a midrate of ARS1.95 to the dollar. Spreads were quoted at ARS1.90 to ARS2.00. Spreads for the interbank rate were ARS2.05/ARS2.07 to the dollar. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders from large banks said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market early Thursday, offering dollars at ARS1.80. The bank has usually offered the dollar at ARS1.65 and has offered them as low as ARS1.60. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dictates that banks that purchase dollars from the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  not mark them up by more than 7% when reselling the dollars. \par \par \uc2  The higher rate also hinted that the bank is out looking for dollars after having tapped into precious foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to prop up the peso. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said that many Argentines are buying dollars head of Saturday's announcement of new economic measures. The government, which is currently gearing up to fully negotiate with the IMF and other lenders for up to $20 billion in aid, may free float the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help clinch the deal. \par \par \uc2  The Fund has been lukewarm on Argentina's use of a dual exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  As for the interbank rate, traders said that companies have been buying dollars to pay bills and buoy their accounts against further devaluation of the peso. \par \par \uc2  Some companies were also seen buying dollars to pay debts after Congress passed a new bankruptcy law after lawmakers scrapped an amendment from the bill prohibiting many companies from transferring money abroad to meet their debt payments. \par \par \uc2  The bill will now go before President Eduardo Duhalde for signature. It isn't clear if Duhalde will sign the bill into law or veto it. \par \par \uc2  Some indebted Argentine companies have blamed bank and wire transfer curbs for not being able to meet their debt obligations. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Darin Liskey, Dow Jones Newswires; (5411) 4314-2757; tom.liskey@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020131dy1v001dj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
