\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2 's Peso -2: Ctrl Bk To Keep  \highlight22\uc2 Intervening \highlight\uc2  - Clarin \b0\par\par\uc2 292 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2002\par 16:19\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to sell dollars indefinitely to keep the peso from depreciating further, Aldo Pignanelli, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  director told Clarin newspaper in an article published Friday. "There are sufficient reserves" to continue selling dollars, Pignanelli said. \par \par \uc2  When Argentina devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Jan. 6, it established a dual exchange rate system. An official rate of ARS1.40 to the dollar is in place for trade, while other transactions will be subject to the floating rate. \par \par \uc2  Pignanelli said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aimed at keeping the free-market peso's value close to the fixed rate. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in continuing efforts to prop up the peso has mandated that banks that buy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars not mark the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up more than five cents, or 7%, when the commercial banks sell the bills. \par \par \uc2  The band, "has given excellent results," to help maintain and improve the peso's price, Pignanelli told Clarin. \par \par \uc2  Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has only been operating for 11 sessions, since the country devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early January. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , selling dollars, in 10 of those sessions. \par \par \uc2  Banking restrictions, in place since early December, have virtually cut off the private sectors' access to some $65 billion in private bank accounts, making cash scarce in the local market. Ordinary Argentines' need for peso notes to pay bills and execute daily activities has also helped the peso from losing excessive ground against the dollar. Argentines in recent weeks have waited in block-long lines outside banks and exchange houses, many using socked away dollars to buy pesos. \par \par \uc2  -By Dawn Weiner, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; dawn.weiner@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020125dy1p000x8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
