\par\par \b \uc2  Argentina Cenbank props peso for sixth day straight. \b0\par\par\uc2 194 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2002\par 15:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jan 22 (Reuters) - The Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the sixth straight session on Tuesday, selling U.S. dollars at 1.65 pesos to prop up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the bank said. \par \par \uc2  The bank did not say how many dollars it sold. \par \par \uc2  The peso closed at an average 1.75/1.80 per dollar for large-scale transactions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday. The 1.80 peso sell rate implies a 44 percent fall from the pre-devaluation one-to-one peso-to-dollar rate that reigned for a decade. \par \par \uc2  Argentina has an official fixed exchange rate of 1.40 pesos per dollar for foreign trade and preferred transactions as designated by the government, and an unofficial floating peso for cash deals. The government hopes to ditch the dual exchange rate and fully float the peso during the second half. \par \par \uc2  The floating-rate peso has weakened as companies and people have sought the safe haven of dollars due to fears that the peso will depreciate even further amid a deep recession now in its fourth year. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020122dy1m00rsn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
