\par\par \b \uc2  IMF To Send Debt, Fiscal Team To Argentina: Krueger \b0\par\par\uc2 406 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2002\par 15:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO -(Dow Jones)- The International Monetary Fund's first deputy managing director Anne Krueger said Friday that  the IMF is preparing a second Argentina technical mission to focus on fiscal and debt issues that could arrive in Buenos Aires as early as next week. \par \par \uc2  The Argentine government has not yet formally requested funding, but does plan to do so, Krueger told reporters at a  press conference before a World Bank seminar in Santiago. \par \par \uc2  An IMF technical mission focusing on banking issues departed for Argentina on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Krueger said the second mission was scheduled to arrive in Argentina early next week, but might now be delayed by a couple of days. \par \par \uc2  Krueger riled members of Argentina's new government last  week, implying that the administration's plan to devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pull the country out of a four-year recession lacked coherence. \par \par \uc2  Until President Eduardo Duhalde's administration devises  a comprehensive and coherent economic strategy "there is no point in our talking about a fund support program for them," she said. \par \par \uc2  The IMF suspended a previous multibillion dollar loan program for Argentina in December. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, Krueger said the lending agency has given Argentina a broad outline of what must be done to regain IMF  support. Argentina must address issues such as restoring the banking system, balancing the budget, and negotiating with creditors some acceptable solution to their debts, she said. Argentina is in default on its $141 billion sovereign debt. \par \par \uc2  Krueger also said, echoing comments made last week, that  Argentina must formulate a new monetary policy based on a unified exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  Argentina officially devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month, cutting the peso loose from a long-standing 1:1 dollar peg and introducing a dual exchange rate regime in an  effort to ease the transition to a floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A fixed exchange rate of ARS1.40 to the dollar will govern large commercial transactions and some financial transactions, while a parallel, free-floating rate was introduced for retail transactions \par \par \uc2  Within a few days of the devaluation, Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prop up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Krueger said Friday that while \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  might make a case for small \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  meant to smooth out fluctuations, large scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market might be questionable. \par \par \uc2  -By Andrea Welsh, Dow Jones Newswires; 562-460-8547; chile@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020118dy1i000x3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
