\par\par \b \uc2  Courts step up bank probe as IMF warns Argentina. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Simon Gardner \par \uc2 756 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2002\par 20:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, Argentina, Jan 18 (Reuters) - Argentine courts on Friday stepped up their probe into the exodus of bank deposits that heralded Argentina's current crisis, while foreign banks and the International Monetary Fund sounded new alarm bells on the state of the battered banking system. \par \par \uc2  "Those bringing the complaint say possible crimes include misappropriation of funds (and) fraud within the public administration," said Norberto Oyarbide, one of the judges heading the probe. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to prop up th - zz~ the International Monetary Fund said heavier \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be "questionable" and warned the government not to expect fresh aid to prop up the banking system soon. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, tension in the streets of Buenos Aires and other big cities was palpable. Police???On Thursday, authorities raided the local offices of London-based HSBC Holdings and Banco Frances , owned by Spain's Banco Bilbao Vizcaya Argentaria . \par \par \uc2  The raids were part of an investigation into the wave of funds sent abroad late last year that led to unpopular government curbs on cash withdrawals. \par \par \uc2  ALARMS SOUNDED \par \par \uc2  With an estimated four out of every five Argentines opposing limits on cash withdrawals, analysts warned that banking restrictions were infuriating foreign-owned banks and threatening to cripple the industry. \par \par \uc2  Spain's biggest bank, Santander Central Hispano , which sources have said could leave Argentina if new restrictions are not revised, said it was "pessimistic" about the situation and called for a "credible" government plan. \par \par \uc2  The Wall Street Journal on Friday reported FleetBoston Financial was considering leaving the country after the government decree that would force banks to convert dollar-denominated loans into devalued pesos. FleetBoston later said it was not planning strategic changes in Argentina. \par \par \uc2  Speaking in neighboring Chile, the IMF's first deputy managing director, Anne Krueger, said there was no money in the pipeline. "It would be very difficult to have anything within a month." she said. \par \par \uc2  Krueger also said that while a case could be made for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "small" \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , any large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be questionable. \par \par \uc2  Even after \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  named a new \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief with close ties to the IMF on Thursday, Krueger's comments suggested the appointment would not pay easy dividends beyond an agreement to delay for a year a $933 million loan repayment. \par \par \uc2  On the Argentine stock market, which reopened on Thursday after being closed for most of the past month, local blue-chip shares soared again on Friday, as investors sought refuge from a plunging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the curbs on cash withdrawals from banks and a partial freeze on savings. The MerVal leading share index was up 10.7 percent by mid-afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The free-floating peso traded at 1.90/1.95 to the dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars at 1.85 pesos, down about 49 percent from the one-to-one rate in effect for the past decade. New President Eduardo Duhalde devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month, days after Argentina defaulted on its $141 billion public debt. \par \par \uc2  The government has said it would ease its bank curbs to allow withdrawals of dollar savings up to $5,000 by first turning them into pesos. \par \par \uc2  But savers can only convert them at the 1.40-peso fixed exchange rate in place for official government transactions and exports - meaning they will lose a big chunk of their savings. And any funds exceeding that limit will remain fixed-term deposits for at least a year. \par \par \uc2  President Eduardo Duhalde, Argentina's fifth president in only two weeks, has described the bank curbs as a "time bomb," but says rising poverty is an even greater evil. About a million people joined the ranks of the poor in the 1990s, and 650,000 fell into poverty around Buenos Aires alone last year. \par \par \uc2  So far, Argentine stores have generally resisted raising prices to offset a near 50 percent decline in the value of the peso, while swank downtown restaurants in the capital - until recently one of the most expensive places in the world - experimented with cheap fixed-price menus. \par \par \uc2  But some multinationals were brutally honest about how the devaluation was going to affect their customers. \par \par \uc2  "Are we going to raise our prices in pesos? Yes," automaker Ford Motor Co. said in a full-page newspaper advertisement on Friday. "(But) we are dropping them 26 percent in dollar terms." \par \par \uc2  "You may insult us, you may agree with us, but you will never be able to say Ford hid when times were tough." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020118dy1i01121\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
