\par\par \b \uc2  Argentine  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  sells dollars to prop up peso. \b0\par\par\uc2 163 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2002\par 15:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jan 18 (Reuters) - The Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the fourth straight day on Friday, selling U.S. dollars at 1.85 pesos to prop up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the bank said. \par \par \uc2  The bank did not say how many dollars it sold. \par \par \uc2  Argentina has a dual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate - an official fixed rate of 1.40 pesos per dollar for foreign trade and preferred transactions as designated by the government, and an unofficial floating peso for cash deals. \par \par \uc2  The floating-rate peso has weakened as companies and people have sought the safe haven of dollars on fears the peso might depreciate even further amid a deep recession now in its fourth year. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the peso traded early Friday at an average 1.90/1.95 per dollar for large-scale transactions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market , compared with Thursday's close at 1.92/1.97. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020118dy1i00skr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
