\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Debt-Drifts lower amid LatAm  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  jitters. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hugh Bronstein \par \uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2002\par 18:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 16 (Reuters) - Emerging market sovereign bond prices drifted slightly lower on Wednesday as investors nervously surveyed the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Latin America and wondered if Argentina's economic crisis may yet throw a wet blanket over the region. \par \par \uc2  "Everything feels heavy. People are just taking profits now," one emerging debt trader said. "The (Brazilian) real and the Chilean peso have been under pressure and now we're starting to see it in Mexico. That's causing some nervousness." \par \par \uc2  While those three \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have bounced off the lows seen early this month, analysts said they remain vulnerable to fallout as recession-plagued Argentina struggles to stabilize its peso. \par \par \uc2  Weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  increase risk for foreign investors and make it more difficult for countries to service their international debt. \par \par \uc2  Brazil C bonds slumped 3/8 to bid 77-3/8, Mexico DISC bonds were flat at a bid of 99 and Argentina FRBS fell 1 to bid 27. \par \par \uc2  "The jitters are over local markets. Yesterday Brazilian bonds followed the real one for one," said Sara Zervos, head of emerging markets fixed income strategy WestLB, the German investment bank. \par \par \uc2  "Brazil and the rest of the local markets in Latin America can still be affected by \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," she said. \par \par \uc2  The Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the second straight day on Wednesday, selling U.S. dollars at 1.70 pesos to prop up the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The bank did not say how many dollars it sold. \par \par \uc2  "The market considers the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be throwing money out the window because there is no way they can defend the exchange rate," Zervos said. "It is very likely they are going to go from a plan of floating the exchange rate in five months to floating it soon. I don't think it will last a month." \par \par \uc2  Argentina has a dual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate - an official fixed rate of 1.40 pesos per dollar for foreign trade and banking transactions, and an unofficial floating peso for cash deals. \par \par \uc2  Debt traders agreed Argentina's new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system is a big source of anxiety now that the government has broken the peso's 10-year link with the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  "There had been the expectation that there might be some relief once the peso started trading," one emerging debt trader said. "But no one believes this exchange rate is real or that there's a legitimate handle on what's going on." \par \par \uc2  Argentina, suffering from a recession now in its fourth year, has defaulted on part of its $141 billion debt and devalued the peso in the last month. \par \par \uc2  SPREADS FLAT \par \par \uc2  Emerging market bond spreads, as measured by JP Morgan's Emerging Markets Bond Index Plus, were more or less flat by midday at 734 basis points over U.S. Treasuries. The bond spreads of Mexico stood one basis point tighter at 320, after a small rally on Tuesday when Fitch upgraded the country's credit rating to investment grade. \par \par \uc2  The move by Fitch increased expectations that Standard & Poor's would promote Mexico to investment grade, as Moody's Investors Service did nearly two years ago. \par \par \uc2  But a top S&P official said Tuesday that the ratings agency would take it's time analyzing Mexico's recently-passed tax reform package and meet with government officials before deciding whether or not to grant an upgrade. \par \par \uc2  Higher credit ratings indicate a reduction in the risk of default. Hence, the higher the rating, the lower the cost of borrowing money. \par \par \uc2  By granting Mexico investment grade status, Fitch and Moody's have confirmed the country's economic integration with its North American neighbors, distinguishing it from most Latin American nations which are still in the "junk bond" category. \par \par \uc2  Many investment houses require portfolio managers to hold bonds that carry the investment grade stamp of approval from Moody's and/or S&P. Fitch is seen by the market as less influential in that regard. \par \par \uc2  "The boost that Mexico got yesterday from Fitch's upgrade was simply because it was seen as increasing the odds of an S&P upgrade," one Wall Street analyst said. "But the S&P upgrade is an open question. It may not happen until the second half of the year." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020116dy1g00ykp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
