\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 1-LatAm markets split - Wall Street slide hits Mexico. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Katherine Baldwin \par \uc2 846 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2001\par 19:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, Brazil, Sept 17 (Reuters) - Latin American markets were split on Monday as a sharp drop on Wall Street on the first day of trading since Tuesday's devastating plane attacks battered Mexican shares while investors snapped up already pummeled stocks in Brazil and Argentina. \par \par \uc2  But players said trading regionwide was erratic and volumes were razor-thin with many investors reluctant to put their capital at risk until the United States' shell-shocked markets set a clearer trend. \par \par \uc2  Mexican stocks, also trading for the first time since hijacked planes demolished New York's World Trade Center and hit the Pentagon near Washington, were off 1.9 percent, following a sharp fall on the Dow Jones Industrial Average .DJI) and the tech-heavy Nasdaq .IXIC). \par \par \uc2  In Argentina and Brazil, stocks that took a beating last week in the wake of the attacks climbed as investors seized cheaply priced companies and cheered the U.S. Federal Reserve's decision earlier Monday to knock 50 basis points off interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "There is a lot of bargain hunting going on," said Walter Molano, Latin American economist at BCP Securities in New York. "But the prices we are seeing on the screens are very preliminary and are not very indicative." \par \par \uc2  MEXICO TRIMS LOSSES AFTER SHARP DROP \par \par \uc2  Mexico's benchmark IPC index .MXX) of 35 most liquid stocks pared losses after initial panic at the outset sent it down about 4.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  But Wall Street's later climb off its worst lows eased jitters in Mexico. By late morning, the IPC, led by media and telecom shares, was trading at 5419.24, or 1.9 percent, below its close in an abbreviated session on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "In (the) short-term we will see a lot of volatility as some bail out of the market, but we are trying to be more objective and are recommending to our clients to stay put as we await a clearer picture," said Rogelio Gallegos, portfolio manager at Actinver in Mexico City. \par \par \uc2  Mexico sends 85 percent of its exports to the United States and its economic fortunes and foreign investment inflows are closely tied to its northern neighbor's economy. \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the Mexican peso MEXO1) firmed 8.65 centavos to 9.4580 against the U.S. dollar from Friday's close, after shedding nearly 2 percent last week. \par \par \uc2  Argentine and Brazilian benchmark bonds moved slightly lower in minute trading volumes. \par \par \uc2  BOTTOM FISHING \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in the region, stocks gained as investors sought for bargains in the wake of last week's sell-off that hit emerging markets particularly hard. Players feared the attacks would accelerate a slowdown in U.S. and global economic growth and tension mounted over a possible military response for the attacks that left thousands of people dead or missing. \par \par \uc2  On Brazil's Sao Paulo Stock Exchange's benchmark Bovespa index .BVSP), the region's largest, pummeled telephone stocks and oil giant Petrobras lifted shares off two-year lows. \par \par \uc2  The index rose 5.1 percent to 10,542 after shedding 18 percent last week to levels not seen since August 1999. Phone companies Telemar and Brasil Telecom rallied. \par \par \uc2  Jetmaker Embraer, however, mirrored the slump in global airline shares, dropping 3.7 percent on fears the U.S. attacks would slash air travel and reduce airlines' profits. Analysts said Continental Airlines' announcement that it would default on some financing agreements and lay off staff pushed Embraer even lower. Continental makes up about 50 percent of Embraer's order book. \par \par \uc2  "The shares are falling on fears Continental Airlines could suspend aircraft orders or cancel orders that were already made," said Rafael Quintanilha, an analyst at BES Securities. \par \par \uc2  But Brazil's real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  BRBY), which has lost more than one-quarter of its value against the dollar this year, was swept up in the buying wave, strengthening to 2.667 reais per dollar from 2.673 reais Friday, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  WIDESPREAD GAINS \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where a recession and financial crisis have been temporarily overshadowed by global market turmoil sparked by the attacks in the United States - the benchmark Merval index .MERV) gained 3.2 percent to 274.470 in wafer-thin trade. \par \par \uc2  "From the market perspective, we are already at lows not seen in a decade, and clearly the moment has come where you have to start thinking about the actual value of shares," said Ruben Pasquali, a trader at Mayoral brokerage. "I don't know if we can logically go down much more." \par \par \uc2  The Merval is already down 35 percent this year. \par \par \uc2  Chilean and Venezuelan shares posted moderate gains, also on bargain hunting, traders said. Chile's benchmark IPCA .IPCA) index added 1.2 percent to 106.78. Caracas' benchmark IBC index .IBC) rose 1.3 percent to 7195.54. \par \par \uc2  The Chilean peso slumped versus the dollar following the U.S. Fed rate cut, opening at 699.50/700, but later recovered after the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010917dx9h00wpw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
