\par\par \b \uc2  Colombia holds rates on Argentina worries-FinMin. \b0\par\par\uc2 144 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2001\par 19:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOGOTA, Colombia, July 13 (Reuters) - Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  opted against lowering its money market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates on Friday due to ongoing economic troubles in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Finance Minister Juan Manuel Santos said. \par \par \uc2  "In the meeting (of \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  directors) we were going to lower rates, but given the situation in Argentina we decided not to lower them," Santos said as he exited the meeting. \par \par \uc2  Fears that Argentina's woes could end in a debt default have rattled markets across the world, battering Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors fret over a potential 1998-style emerging market meltdown. \par \par \uc2  Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not lowered rates since March 12 - when it cut all money market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates by 50 basis points. Analysts have said a rate cut would have helped spur growth in Latin America's fifth largest economy. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx7d07iuc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
