\par\par \b \uc2  Chile's Peso Ends Weaker At 665.70/Dlr, Back From 674.20 \b0\par\par\uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2001\par 19:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO -(Dow Jones)- Chile's peso ended at a record weak point of CLP665.70 against the dollar Thursday, after hitting an historic intraday low of CLP674.20 at the open on fears that Argentina's economic crisis will end in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation or debt default. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the peso bounced back from its intraday low on mounting expectations that Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will make another move to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after temporarily halting the peso's slide by announcing plans last week to float $1 billion in debt on local financial markets. \par \par \uc2  Despite the modest rebound, the peso ended 0.3% weaker against the dollar than Wednesday's CLP664.00 close, and 13.8% weaker from the beginning of the year. Economists attribute the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's steady depreciation largely to contagion effects from Argentina. \par \par \uc2  Traders attributed the peso's early decline to lack of confidence in economic emergency measures announced by Argentine President Fernando de la Rua and Economy Minister Domingo Cavallo. The pair promised late Wednesday to balance Argentina's budget by making severe spending cuts and enlisting banks to improve tax collection. \par \par \uc2  Traders in Chile said the peso began to rebound in afternoon trading, as growing expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  stemmed the rush to dollars. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dead. It's no longer the relevant point now that the peso is at the 670-level," said one local trader, adding that market players were pinning their hopes for \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action on a monetary policy meeting scheduled for late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Many economists, though, are still cautious about guessing what action the Bank might take. \par \par \uc2  Jose Ramon Valente, an economist with local consultancy Econsult, shrugged off market talk that the Bank will either tap its reserves to pump dollars directly into the market or double back on a six-month easing bent to raise its benchmark interbank lending rate from 3.50%. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank's best option is to intensify the measure taken last week and increase the quantity of dollar-denominated bonds it will place," said Valente. "The Bank should also give a strong signal to the market that the situation in Argentina is short-term, and that buying dollars (at the current exchange rate) could be risky," he added. \par \par \uc2  The Bank is expected to adjourn its monthly policy meeting after markets close Thursday. \par \par \uc2  -By Andrea Welsh, Dow Jones Newswires; 562-460-8547; chile@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1170 1844 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010712dx7c0010q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
