\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's Fraga: Ctrl Bk's Hands Not Tied On  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Policy \b0\par\par\uc2 970 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2001\par 22:17\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil President Arminio Fraga said Wednesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market beyond the $6 billion the bank recently earmarked for spot market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  "We haven't tied our hands at all," Fraga said at a news conference. "We can do (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly or through other instruments," such as dollar-linked bonds. \par \par \uc2  Fraga noted that the $6 billion is designed to "irrigate" the market and smooth the country's balance of payments. \par \par \uc2  "The spot market we feel needs a little clarity and we're going to stick to $6 billion," he said. \par \par \uc2  Asked if more could be spent on the spot market if conditions warrant, he said, "It's not our intention to change but I don't think I want to go as far as saying never." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unveiled its new \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans last week, saying it would spend roughly $50 million a day on the spot market. \par \par \uc2  Market participants question whether the $6 billion figure would be enough to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly given the ongoing volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They also criticized the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for specifying its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy, as it could spark speculation against the real. \par \par \uc2  Despite several \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the plan was announced, the real has continued to lose ground. On Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit an all-time low of BRR2.570 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Fraga is in New York for a series of meetings with investors and bankers. He said the goal of the meetings is to confirm the commitment of Brazilian officials to economic stability and to explain the country's policy approach amid the market turmoil. \par \par \uc2  The real recovered from its earlier lows to close at BRR2.50 per dollar Wednesday, following a direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the spot market and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sale of dollar-linked debt. The finishing mark was, however, the weakest ever close. \par \par \uc2  Fraga downplayed the market criticism of the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, saying it's impossible to determine how effective the new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan has been, but that it's also impossible to know what would have happened if the measure had not been announced. \par \par \uc2  Fraga, who spoke to 80 market participants at a luncheon sponsored by J.P. Morgan, said that the response among investors has been favorable, noting that many had doubts about the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  "He really clarified a lot with respect to what they're doing on the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said one fund manager for a European bank, who asked not to be identified. "I would say I'm a bit more confident" in how the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is handling things. \par \par \uc2  Fraga, for his part, said the reforms put in place over the last two years have prepared Brazil to weather the current turbulence caused by weakness in Argentina and an energy crisis at home. \par \par \uc2  "While I think the short-term problems we are going through are very serious, I also feel very strongly that all the policies we have put in place...have given us a chance to look at these problems as temporary problems we will be able to address," Fraga said. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  projects economic growth of 2.8% in 2001, down from 4.5% last year. This year's figure was originally expected to surpass 4%, but an energy crisis has led economists to slash their forecasts. \par \par \uc2  As for inflation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently projected a 5.8% rate for 2001. That's near the top end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range for the year of between 2% and 6%. \par \par \uc2  Fraga declined to comment on whether the continued weakening of the real had further eroded the outlook for inflation, saying that the matter would be studied at next week's monetary policy committee meeting. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which has raised its key rate a cumulative 300 basis points since March - is widely expected to have to hike rates next week given the surge in inflationary pressure caused by the weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Fraga was hesitant to express much optimism about Argentina's prospects for recovery, though he said he welcomed news that Argentina was planning to implement spending cuts. \par \par \uc2  Argentine Economy Minister Domingo Cavallo is expected to soon announce austerity measures. \par \par \uc2  "I see Argentina trying to respond to its difficulties," Fraga said. "The feeling I have is that they want to get things done." \par \par \uc2  Asked about the impact of increased bickering between Argentina and Brazil on the trade front, Fraga said he wasn't particularly concerned. \par \par \uc2  Early last week, Argentina announced that it had cut import tariffs for certain products coming from countries outside the Mercosur trade bloc - which includes Argentina, Brazil, Paraguay and Uruguay. Brazil said the measures violated existing agreements and said it would suspend bilateral trade talks with Argentina. \par \par \uc2  "During these last few years, with problems in Brazil and now in Argentina, I think it's natural that you get this sort of discussion," Fraga said. "We're committed to staying at the table, talking through each case." \par \par \uc2  "I'm confident that that won't be a major issue in this picture at all," he added. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere on the trade front, Fraga said Washington could help out Brazil by committing to opening up the U.S. market to Brazilian products. \par \par \uc2  "We feel in Brazil very strongly that the model we have been developing requires open markets for our products," Fraga said. "We're ready to sit at the table. We need a clear signal from the American government that this is for real, that we're gonna open markets." \par \par \uc2  -By Stephen Wisnefski, Dow Jones Newswires; (201) 938-5692; stephen.wisnefski@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010711dx7b00c6y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
