\par\par \b \uc2  Chile may take measures to soften Argentine impact. \b0\par\par\uc2 644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2001\par 22:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  VALPARAISO, Chile, July 11 (Reuters) - The Chilean government said on Wednesday it might take extra economic measures to counter the harmful effect on the local economy of an economic and political crisis in neighboring Argentina. \par \par \uc2  The main impact in Chile of Argentina's chronic instability has been a steady depreciation of the Chilean peso . The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sank to a fresh historic low on Wednesday of 664 pesos to the dollar, losing more than 13 percent of its value against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year. \par \par \uc2  "We do not rule out any measure," Finance Minister Nicolas Eyzaguirre told reporters, without specifying what steps it was considering. \par \par \uc2  "If there are speculative tendencies that bring the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to levels beyond what the fundamentals of the Chilean economy can withstand, then the authorities will have a say in the matter," he added. \par \par \uc2  Regional and global financial markets were rattled on Wednesday by renewed fears that Argentine will default on its massive debt or devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid a worsening political and financial environment. \par \par \uc2  Argentine Economy Minister Domingo Cavallo vowed on Tuesday to shrink the country's hefty budget deficit to zero to pull it out of a three-year recession and restore investor confidence. \par \par \uc2  Last week, the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is independent of the government, said it would inject $1 billion into the market over the next six months via by issuing dollar-denominated promissory notes to help shore up the peso, pummeled by uncertainty over \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It was the institution's first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since it free-floated the peso in 1999. \par \par \uc2  The peso firmed slightly on the news before tumbling again on Argentina's ongoing quandary. \par \par \uc2  SOME EXPECT DOLLAR SALES TO BOLSTER PESO \par \par \uc2  Local market players speculate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next move will be a more aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  along the lines taken by its Brazilian counterpart, selling dollars directly in the market to prop up the peso. \par \par \uc2  To date, however, the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shown no signs of taking that route. \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian real, also driven to record lows on Argentina, has continued to slide despite repeated attempts by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars on the market to break its fall. \par \par \uc2  "I think it is often inconvenient to try to manipulate the market," said Eyzaguirre. "But I also said very clearly that we are not going to let ourselves be manipulated by the market." \par \par \uc2  The Argentine crisis has spilled over into Chile at a time when the former star economy of the region struggles to regain the average 7 percent growth rates it enjoyed through most of the 1990s. \par \par \uc2  Chile grew 5.4 percent last year but a slowdown this year prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lower its 2001 growth forecast to 4.3 percent from 5.6 percent. It sees inflation this year at 3.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  Low prices for copper, Chile's top export, and a global slowdown could lead to further cuts in growth estimates. \par \par \uc2  But Eyzaguirre said the domestic and regional economic troubles do not warrant a dramatic drop in the peso, blaming the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility on excessive market speculation. \par \par \uc2  The government fears a sharp decline in the peso could fuel inflation, which has been capped by sluggish spending in the first half of the year. \par \par \uc2  "We say very clearly that in the event the price of the dollar gets out of hand and this has an impact on inflation, obviously we would not rule out any measure," Eyzaguirre said. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold its monthly monetary policy meeting to assess whether to alter its benchmark lending rate or maintain it at its current annualized rate of 3.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  The institution has cut rates five times this year to kick-start consumer demand, seen as holding back growth. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010711dx7b012ld\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
