\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Debt-Sentiment sagging depsite technical bounce. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Genevieve Wilkinson \par \uc2 542 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 2001\par 23:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, July 6 (Reuters) - Emerging market bonds on Friday rose for the first time in nine trading sessions in a technical bounce, but confidence was frail amid political strife in Argentina and concern that Brazil's efforts to prop up its flagging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were failing. \par \par \uc2  "There is continued political noise in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  and uncertainties about the effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts announced in Brazil," said Lacey Gallagher, director of Latin American research at Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  The J.P. Morgan Emerging Markets Index, which on Thursday lost 2.6 percent, widened three basis points to 809 basis points over U.S. Treasuries. \par \par \uc2  Technical analysts said Argentine FRBs and Brazil C bonds were "deeply oversold" on Thursday. On Friday, the FRB rose 3 percent to 77.750 points, and the C bond rose three-quarters of a point to 72.625 points. \par \par \uc2  "External debt markets are up purely on the back of shortcovering. Higher prices do not indicate a change in sentiment and people are squaring their books before they go home," a head emerging market trader said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the Brazilian real ended firmer on Friday after a yo-yo session in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars to boost the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it plumbed record lows against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The real, which is off 24.8 percent for the year, ended at 2.435 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  IMPASSE BROKEN \par \par \uc2  An impasse between the Argentine federal government and province leaders was broken on Thursday when the government agreed to hand over $600 million to the provinces in exchange for a series of spending cuts. \par \par \uc2  The Buenos Aires province will cut $500 million, the other 11 provinces will cut an additional $150 million, and the federal government itself will slash $150 million off its expenditure sheet. \par \par \uc2  "There's still not consensus across the political spectrum in terms of an economic policy approach so the agreement with the governors beginning with the Province of Buenos Aires - and we expect more - is positive but it's not the only political issue." \par \par \uc2  Michael Gavin, head of Latin American research at UBS Warburg, said the main risk in Argentina relates to whether the current market panic translates into a financial one leading to sharp declines in bank deposits. \par \par \uc2  "Deposits in the banking system have been relatively stable recently and ... there has been no indication of a deposit run amid (Thursday's) panic ... we think yesterday's price action was more a reflection of panic selling than a response to any specific news," Gavin said. \par \par \uc2  Argentina's four-day peso-denominated interbank lending rate hit 20 percent on Friday ahead of a long weekend - Argentina is closed on Monday for Independence Day - in another sign of growing discontent with the country's inability to turn around ten straight quarters of economic contraction. \par \par \uc2  And Economy Minister Domingo Cavallo in Rome announced changes to the $850 million Letes auction, cutting the maturity of the domestic bonds from one year to 90 days. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, emerging market star Mexico, with one foot in the full investment grade door, succumbed to the pessimism as its benchmark 2026 global bond fell 0.750 points to 124.375 - its lowest level since May 30. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010706dx76005vd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
