\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Debt-Shortcovering bounce yet sentiment shaky. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Genevieve Wilkinson \par \uc2 535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 2001\par 16:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, July 6 (Reuters) - Benchmark emerging market bonds on Friday rose for the first time in nine trading sessions in a technical bounce, but confidence was frail in the absence of political consensus toward economic policy approach in Argentina and amid concern that Brazil's efforts to prop up its flagging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were failing. \par \par \uc2  "There is continued political noise in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  and uncertainties about the effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts announced in Brazil," said Lacey Gallagher, director of Latin American research at Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  The J.P. Morgan Emerging Markets Index, which on Thursday lost 2.6 percent, widened three basis points to 809 basis points over U.S. Treasuries. \par \par \uc2  Technical analysts say Argentine FRBs and Brazil C bonds were "deeply oversold" on Thursday. On Friday, the FRB rose a full point to bid around 76.500 and the C bond inched 0.625 points higher to bid around 72.500 points. \par \par \uc2  "External debt markets are up purely on the back of shortcovering. Higher prices do not indicate a change in sentiment and people are squaring their books before they go home," a head emerging market trader said. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, the Brazilian real fell to record lows early on Friday, trading at 2.560 per dollar. The slump came despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement on Thursday that it would spend $6 billion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by the end of the year to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has so far this year lost 31 percent of its value. \par \par \uc2  IMPASSE BROKEN \par \par \uc2  An impasse between the Argentine federal government and province leaders was broken on Thursday when the government agreed to hand over $600 million to the provinces in exchange for a series of spending cuts. \par \par \uc2  The Buenos Aires province will cut $500 million, the other 11 provinces will cut an additional $150 million, and the federal government itself will slash $150 million off its expenditure sheet. \par \par \uc2  "There's still not consensus across the political spectrum in terms of an economic policy approach so the agreement with the governors beginning with the Province of Buenos Aires - and we expect more - is positive but it's not the only political issue." \par \par \uc2  Michael Gavin, head of Latin American research at UBS Warburg, said the main risk in Argentina relates to whether the current market panic translates into a financial one leading to sharp declines in bank deposits. \par \par \uc2  "Deposits in the banking system have been relatively stable recently and ... there has been no indication of a deposit run amid (Thursday's) panic ... we think yesterday's price action was more a reflection of panic selling than a response to any specific news," Gavin said. \par \par \uc2  UBS holds a neutral position on Argentine bonds - meaning the bank recommends investors should hold the same ratio of Argentine bonds as allocated in the EMBI-plus at 21 percent: "But we advise investors to position for a technically driven relief rally in the days ahead," Gavin said. \par \par \uc2  Even emerging market star Mexico, with one foot in the full investment grade door, succumbed to wave of selling in Latin America. The 2026 bond fell 0.875 points to bid around 124.250 points. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010728dx7600sbm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
