\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's Real Ends Weaker Despite Ctrl Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 503 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 21:30\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- The downward slope for Brazil's real steepened Thursday on mounting worries of continuing recession in \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 , leading the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market for the first time in as many times to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening. \par \par \uc2  Concern that newly-appointed Argentine Economy Ministry Ricardo Lopez Murphy may not be able to pull that country out of a three-year recession drove the real to a new 24-month closing low of BRR2.096 to the dollar, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The weakening was amplified by fears that Brazil's trade deficit will widen in coming months after two explosions earlier today halted production at a key oil rig off the coast of Rio de Janeiro. Brazil may have to spend an additional $50 million monthly on oil imports until the rig resumes production. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  entered the market selling dollars a couple of hours after the real crossed the key psychological barrier of BRR2.10/dollar for the first time since March 1999. The real strengthened to BRR2.074 almost instantaneously before losing ground again. \par \par \uc2  "The outlook hinges on the situation in Argentina," said a trader. "If things don't improve there, the real won't recover." \par \par \uc2  The real has lost 6.5% of its value since the start of the year amid concerns over the global economic slowdown and Argentina. The continued weakening is now raising concern that inflation may rise and upset the government's 4% target this year. \par \par \uc2  "If the real remains too weak, inflation may rise as companies will seek to pass on to consumers their increased costs," said Simone Passini, an economist with UBS Warburg in Rio de Janeiro. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to sell dollars on the market was welcome by traders and analysts, who said the real was weakening amid thin liquidity on speculation, rather than on concrete facts. \par \par \uc2  "This kind of movement, which is purely speculative, is bad for the market because it contaminates expectations," said Passini, referring to the impact of the real's weakening on other asset prices. \par \par \uc2  A clear example was the sharp drop in Treasury bond prices on the secondary market. The yield on Treasury bills due Feb. 2002, the most liquid federal bond, rose 27 basis points to 17.03%. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark short-term rate currently stands at 15.25%. \par \par \uc2  The weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will also have a negative impact on public accounts, widening the country's fiscal deficit. Brazil has foreign debt totaling some BRR102 billion and dollar-indexed domestic debt of BRR120 billion. \par \par \uc2  Marcelo Carvalho, chief economist at J.P. Morgan Chase in Sao Paulo, says that, as a rule of thumb, a 10% rise in the real's quote against the dollar will translate into a fiscal cost of roughly 2% of gross domestic product over 12 months. \par \par \uc2  By Adriana Arai, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11 3813-1988 adriana.arai@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020010711dx3f002x4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
