\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Market ADRs Mixed On US Mkts; Latin Amer Down \b0\par\par\uc2 598 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 1999\par 18:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Latin American emerging market shares trading as American depositary receipts were down Thursday as U.S. markets were pressured from various directions. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Asian issues were primarily stronger on gains posted by local markets.. \par \par \uc2  At 12:05 p.m. EDT (1605 GMT), the Dow Jones Industrial Average was down 86.11 to 10905.27. \par \par \uc2  "Everybody seems to be watching each other," a New York-based trader said in reference to market participants' attempts to cite a source for weaknesses in the New York Stock Exchange and U.S. treasurys. \par \par \uc2  News of a record U.S. trade gap hit came out Thursday, the Japanese yen continued to rise against the dollar, and a 25 basis point interest rate hike was expected to ensue from the Aug. 24 meeting of the U.S. Federal Reserve Open Market Committee. \par \par \uc2  Already negative sentiments, combined with light volumes, cast a larger-than-life shadow on the market, and left participants searching for a culprit, traders said. \par \par \uc2  At 11:50 a.m. EDT (1550 GMT), Mexican bellwether Telmex (TMX) was down 1/2 to $74, and Tubos de Acero de Mexico (TAM) was off 1/8 to $11 1/2. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's Telebras HOLDRs (TBH) were down 7/8 to $69 1/8, Tele Centro Sul (TCS) was declining 11/16 to $49 3/4, Telesp Participacoes (TSP) was slipping 1/16 to $15 3/4, Tele Sudeste Celular Participacoes (TSD) was down 3/16 to $20 7/16, and Compania Siderurgica Nacional (SID) was dropping 1 1/8 to $21 1/8. \par \par \uc2  Latin American equities traders expressed concern over rising consumer prices in Brazil and a weakening Brazilian real. These uncertainties have led to some shifts in funds, while putting the country into the spotlight, traders said. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began taking measures Wednesday to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and equities markets. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Banco de Galicia (BGALY) was shedding 3/16 to $17 1/2, Telefonica de Argentina SA (TAR) was down 1/4 to $28 7/16, and Banco Frances (BFR) was off 1/8 to $16 7/8. \par \par \uc2  Chilean issues were propped up in earlier trading by positive sentiments on Embotelladora Andina's Wednesday acquisition announcement of a Brazilian Coca-Cola bottler, but soon the shares were swept down with the market flow. \par \par \uc2  Andina's series B shares (AKOB) were off 1/16 to $13 15/16 while its series A shares (AKOA) were down 1/4 to $17 1/2. Compania de Telecommunicaciones de Chile (CTC) was off 7/16 to $22 1/2, and Gener SA (CHR) was dropping 1/4 to $18 1/4. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Asian ADRs were mostly trading up. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's HSBC Holdings PLC (HBC) were gaining 1 13/16 to $61 3/4, Shanghai Petrochemical (SHI) was up 11/16 to $19 3/8, and China Telecom (CHL) was soaring 2 7/8 to $60 1/2. \par \par \uc2  South Korean blue-chip POSCO (PKX) was up 1 to $33, Korea Telecom Corp. (KTC) was rising 1/16 to $33, and Korea Electric Power Co. (KEP) was gaining 1/4 to $15 3/4. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan Semiconductor (TSM) was off 1/8 to $28 1/2. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Singapore's Pacific Internet Ltd. (PCNTF) was down 1 to $30 1/2, while\par Philippine Long Distance Telephone (PHI) was down 3/4 to $23 3/8.\par -By Amy Guthrie; 201-938-2225; amy.guthrie@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 19-08-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1648GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1836 5076 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv8j065u6\par }\page {\