\par\par \b \uc2  Argentina to enter "most uncertain year of decade". \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alejandra Labanca \par \uc2 636 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 1998\par 20:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, Dec 29 (Reuters) - Argentina's economy is entering 1999 at a slow pace after the global financial storm swamped growth last year and blew clouds of uncertainty over emerging markets, though analysts say strong fundamentals bode well for a robust recovery once the panorama clears. \par \par \uc2  Private forecasts of Argentine economic growth range from zero to 3 percent, a sharp drop from the 4.8 percent expected for this year and the heady 8.6 percent growth registered the previous year. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say 1999 could hold some unpleasant surprises since the root causes of the external shocks that hit Argentina during 1998 have not yet disappeared. \par \par \uc2  Economist Miguel Angel Broda said 1999 could mark "the most uncertain year of the decade. Economic performance will depend on four variables: the speed at which capital flows recover, the rhythm of growth of domestic credit, Brazil and politics." \par \par \uc2  The massive withdrawal of capital from emerging markets after Russia's debt default last August was a nightmare for Argentina, whose economic system is highly dependent on inflows of capital. \par \par \uc2  After almost 50 years of state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and regulation of the economy, \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  adopted a liberal model in the early 1990s, opening up the economy, dismantling price regulation and privatising state companies. The change has boosted economic growth but put the economy at the mercy of international capital flows. \par \par \uc2  The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system requires the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to match money in circulation with dollar reserves, and a debt burden of nearly 30 percent of gross domestic product raises the need for nearly constant refinancing. \par \par \uc2  As a result of the lack of liquidity in international markets during 1998, Argentine interest rates rocketed. That contributed to a trimming of 1998 growth estimates to 4.8 percent from the 5.3 percent forecast by the government early this year. \par \par \uc2  The drop in economic activity meant fiscal revenues came in under expectations, leading the government to overshot a 1998 fiscal deficit target set as part of an agreement with the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  Neighbouring Brazil, Latin America's largest economy, has proved an unsettling source of instability, too. Strong trade ties and geographic proximity assure that Argentina and Brazil will remain closely linked in the minds of investors. In Argentina's case, that can be dangerous when capital get jumpy. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the still-cautious mood of foreign investors and uncertainties about the success of Brazil's fiscal reforms will continue to weigh on Argentina next year. \par \par \uc2  Politically, 1999 is a key year for Argentina. It will mark the first time in 10 years that the Argentine people will not have the option of voting for Carlos Menem, who since 1989 has billed himself as the father of economic prosperity. \par \par \uc2  He was reelected in 1995 largely because of his economic successes but cannot run for a third straight term. \par \par \uc2  This time the opposition Alliance is seen by most opinion polls as the most likely winner of the October presidential election. That has heightened caution among investors, even though Alliance leaders have repeatedly vowed they will not tamper with current economic policies. \par \par \uc2  "Investors will take their time after the change of government. They will wait until March or April 2000 to see what happens," private consultant Orlando Ferreres said. \par \par \uc2  Still, Argentina's fundamentals are strong and the economy is likely to respond quickly and positively to any hint of recovery in Brazil, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  "A period of much-slower growth lies ahead. Both investment and consumer spending are set to weaken," according to a report by Dresdner Kleinwort Benson. "Nonetheless, given its strong fundamentals, Argentina will be an early beneficiary of an eventual change in sentiment towards Brazil." \par \par \uc2  ((Buenos Aires bureau, 541 318-0654, buenosaires.newsroom@reuters.com)))). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926duct01rva\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
