\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2 's Peso Hovers Near Floor On Dlr; No  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 182 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 1995\par 19:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES (AP-DJ)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday for a fourth straight day as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hovered near its floor on the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The peso was trading at 0.9999 peso to the dollar, unchanged from Monday and just above its floor of 1.0000 peso established by a 1991 law that pegged the peso one-to-one with the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The trader at one Argentine bank said state banks like Banco de la Nacion and Banco de la Provinica de Buenos Aires were seen offering funds in order to help out financial institutions suffering a recent liquidity squeeze provoked by the Mexican monetary turmoil. 'It's sort of an indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' he said. \par \par \uc2  The action pushed interbank interest rates lower. The overnight rate on peso-denominated loans between first-line banks, or call money, eased to 13% from 16% on Monday. Call money in dollars held stable at 8.5%. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 10-01-95 \par \par \uc2  1840GMT \par \par \uc2  1157 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr1a002xl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
